Z||000005||Oreif||04-18-2001||10:07 AM||||The Fiero Price guide which is done by Paul Vargyas, Is what average prices Fiero's are being sold for. I have noticed that the prices are slowly creeping up. Not a Lot but a little. According to all the older copies I have generally Fieros have increased about $500 over the past 4 years. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Dave Gunsul||04-18-2001||09:04 PM||||Here in IL. there's a guy selling a 93 mile,<BR>not 93,000 not 9,300 but 93 mile, yellow 88<BR>GT with leather for $18,500!!<BR>He has started it over the years and changed<BR>the fluids but hasn't driven it except home<BR>from the dealer on May 11th 1988!<BR>He also has (not for sale) an 87 GT with<BR>black ground effects that he had put on on the assembly line!! This is a one of a kind<BR>GT with the papers to prove it! <BR>I've seen both and they are PERFECT!<P>Sorry about these ramblings but this thread just got me thinking about them. :-)<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000007||PAUL88GT||04-18-2001||10:42 PM||||With my 86se restoration, I am finding out that individual parts are expensive, but you seem to be able to buy entire cars in rough condition at reasonable prices.  This situation makes it almost impossible to restore a fiero and sell it for a profit.  You just do it for a the love of the car and to get an enjoyable car to drive. But the currrent market will cause a lot of restorable fieros to get parted out for profit.  Scuse my ramblings.<P>Paul  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Spektyr||04-18-2001||10:50 PM||||There aren't really any cars you can restore for profit.  Unless you can find someone that doesn't know the value of their car and pick it up for an insanely low price.<P>Whether you're going for pure restoration, or resto-mod, you have to assume you're going to spend a lot more money 'finishing' the car than someone will pay you for it.<P>And in the case where the individual parts are expensive (like Fiero's) and the cars can be had relatively cheaply, a parts car is the way to go (assuming you have the room).<P>One other thing... 500 bucks in four years isn't a price climb... it's just keeping pace with inflation.  But then again, most cars can't keep pace.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Galen||04-18-2001||11:55 PM||||a 93 miles GT is worth the same to me as a 93k mile.. heh.. prolly less.. can't stand ppl not driving cars.. thats what they were made for, not meant to be locked up in a garage..<P>------------------<BR>Galen - 1988 Hawaiian Orchid Coupe - <A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero Website</A><BR><A HREF="http://www.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>Spinrpg.com - Free URL & e-mail Forwarding</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cheever2||04-19-2001||02:13 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>500 bucks in four years isn't a price climb... it's just keeping pace with inflation<HR></BLOCKQUOTE>"Well, that would be <I>more</I>, wouldn't it?!" - John Lovitz, <I>A League of Their Own</I><P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Spektyr||04-19-2001||12:06 PM||||Yeah, yeah, yeah, x + 500 is greater than x.<P>But you have to keep in mind the difference between 1980's dollars and 2001 dollars.<P>For instance, what was the sticker price on a new 1965 Mustang.  2500 or so?  And a fully restored Stang goes for 8-10k easily (assuming it isn't rare or special in some way).  Does that mean that it has appreciated?  No.  A new Mustang goes for 20k roughly.  Now while I'd much rather have a 65 Mustang than a new one, the new Stang is actually worth more in terms of dollars.  I have a 50cent piece I carry with me all the time that was minted in my birth year.  I've had it for over 10 years, and I wouldn't sell it for anything resembling it's face value.  However, while it may be worth more than 50cents to me, it's still only worth 50cents in real world dollars.<P>That in a nutshell defines the phenomenon we see on a daily basis: "I've put over $15,000 dollars into this car, must sacrifice, asking $8,500".  These cars are worth it to US to rebuild.  We love them, we pamper them, we spend every dollar we can making them perfect.  But when it comes time to sell them, you just don't get your money back.<P>Investors typically will not consider anything that does not give them 10% return on their investment per year.  The increase in Fiero prices you're talking about doesn't represent an appreciation, just a much slower than normal depreciation.<P>I'm not saying that it isn't impressive.  Most cars loose value every year in a purely numerical sense (not counting the inflation of the dollar.)  For any car to show a purely numerical increase is impressive.  But the fact of the matter remains: the 500 dollar increase is actually below the increase in 'value' of the dollar, or below inflation.  So while the numerical figure increases, the dollar is increasing faster creating a net loss.<P>But you'll have a hard time finding too many Pontiacs that can hold value like a Fiero.<P>Think of it this way.  A boat traveling upstream at 15mph against a 16mph current is actually moving downstream at 1mph.  But it's holding it's ground a hell of a lot better than all the other stalled out boats going downstream at 16mph.||||reg||1
